  "body": "## Problem\n\nThe gate stall watchdog (#3303, PR #3489) kills a stalled step by its process tree. It reads the tree by parent pid (`ps` on Unix, `Win32_Process` on Windows), then kills each pid (`kill -KILL`, `taskkill /F /T`). This has three gaps:\n\n- Between the read of the process list and the kill, a pid can be used again by a new process. The kill can then hit a wrong process. The window is small.\n- A process that left the tree (a double fork, `setsid`) is not killed.\n- In the capture path of `direct::cargo` (`xtask/src/direct.rs`), such a process can keep the stdout pipe open. Then `reader.join()` waits until that process ends.\n\n## Change\n\n- Unix: start each step child in its own process group (`std::os::unix::process::CommandExt::process_group(0)`), and kill the group (`kill -KILL -<pgid>`).\n- Windows: put each step child in a job object, and end the job.\n- Keep the present tree read for the list of the processes that were alive.\n\n## Done means\n\n- `xtask/src/watchdog.rs` kills by process group or job object.\n- A test shows that a grandchild that left the tree by `setsid` (Unix) dies with the step.\n- `cargo test -p xtask` and `cargo clippy -p xtask --all-targets -- -D warnings` are green.\n\nRefs #3303\n",
